course: dictionary
lesson: 5649
title: The lipogram: a writing game
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A lipogram is a piece of writing in which one letter of the alphabet is never used on purpose. The writer sets themselves a rule, such as a story with no letter "e", and must then find other words to say what they mean.
explain: «A lipogram leaves out one letter on purpose; "e" is the most common letter in English, so a lipogram without "e" is the hardest.» Here "most common" means the letter you see most often. Leaving out a letter you see often makes the challenge bigger.
explain: Lipogram is a noun, and a writer can write a lipogram. In English lessons and word puzzles it is a game of choosing words carefully. In olympiad puzzles you may be asked to spot which letter is missing.
fact: A lipogram is writing that never uses one chosen letter, on purpose.
fact: «E» is the most common letter in English, so a lipogram without «e» is the hardest classic challenge.
fact: Writers use lipograms to practise finding other words and to see how often letters appear.
fact: In olympiad puzzles you may be asked to spot which letter is missing.
exercise: What is a lipogram? | *A piece of writing that leaves out one letter on purpose | A piece of writing with one letter repeated by mistake | A very long word | A sum with numbers only
exercise: «A lipogram without «e» is the hardest classic challenge.» Which letter is left out? | q | *e | z | x
exercise: Why is a lipogram without «e» the hardest? | Because «e» is the hardest letter to write | Because «e» is the first letter of the alphabet | *Because «e» is the most common letter in English | Because «e» is never used in any word
test: Which letter is the most common in English, so the hardest to leave out? | *e | o | a | t
test: What does a writer do when writing a lipogram? | Writes in a very large font | *Writes without using one chosen letter, on purpose | Writes with one letter used very often | Writes numbers instead of words
exam: In a lipogram, how is the missing letter chosen? | *On purpose, by a rule the writer sets | By the teacher each morning | By accident, when they forget the letter | By a computer that picks letters at random
